     Folio-case close-to-sleep / open-to-wake.

     WHY THIS FILE AND NOT AN SW_LID KEYLAYOUT
     ─────────────────────────────────────────
     There is no SW_LID on this device and there is none on stock either. The
     cover magnet is a ROHM BU52053NVX hall switch wired to GPIO 135 and owned
     by the Qualcomm sensor DSP, so it surfaces only as an Android Sensor:

       dumpsys sensorservice
       0x3fd) bu52053nvx Hall Effect Sensor Non-wakeup | ROHM | qti.sensor.hall_effect(33171002)
       0x3fe) bu52053nvx Hall Effect Sensor Wakeup     | ROHM | qti.sensor.hall_effect(33171002)

     Nothing in /dev/input carries it. The three decoys were each ruled out by
     disassembling the shipped modules (their md5s match the factory image):

       - hall_irq (event5, key codes 750-757). pen1/2/3_hall_irq_func contain
         no input_event call at all — they drive lenovo_qi_update_hall, i.e.
         pen wireless charging. Only pogo_hall_irq_func emits, codes 756/757,
         and that is keyboard *dock* attach/detach. /proc/interrupts shows the
         pen IRQs fired once each at probe and no pogo IRQ is even registered.
       - novatek,hall-gpio (the same GPIO 135). nvt_hall_check_func polls only
         while suspended and on change does a single CTP_SPI_WRITE(0x1150);
         it is touch/gesture suppression, and reports no input event.
       - keyboard scancodes 654/655. Stock's own keylayout names these
         KEYBOARD_RESTORE / KEYBOARD_REVERSE — the keyboard slab folding back,
         not the cover. See keylayout/Vendor_17ef_Product_62b2.kl.

     Stock consumes the sensor from com.zui.server.policy.ZuiPhoneWindowManager
     inside its patched services.jar and feeds the result straight into AOSP's
     own InputManagerService.notifyLidSwitchChanged(). We cannot patch the
     framework, but AOSP already exposes the same behaviour declaratively:
     DeviceStateProviderImpl reads this file from /vendor/etc/devicestate/
     (VENDOR_CONFIG_FILE_PATH, DeviceStateProviderImpl.java:104-106) and
     LogicalDisplayMapper turns the resulting states into sleep/wake
     (shouldDeviceBeWoken / shouldDeviceBePutToSleep, :642-700).

     POLARITY — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
     ─────────────────────────────────
     Three independent confirmations that value 1 = open, 0 = closed:

       1. ZuiPhoneWindowManager.mHallSensorListener, decompiled from the
          factory system/framework/services.jar:
              int i = (int) sensorEvent.values[0];
              if (i == 1) farTriggerLidSwitch();   // far  -> cover open
              else        nearTriggerLidSwitch();  // near -> cover closed
       2. It is an active-low hall switch, so magnet present pulls the line low.
       3. The owner worked the cover through two full cycles while GPIO 135 was
          polled at 5 Hz (work/s13/folio.log). It tracked all four transitions
          with no misses and no bounce:
              t= 0.00  HIGH  cover open
              t=34.83  LOW   closed
              t=51.32  HIGH  opened
              t=68.96  LOW   closed
              t=88.95  HIGH  opened

     WHY THE SENSOR PATH IS REQUIRED FOR *WAKE* SPECIFICALLY
     ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The same capture shows keyboard scancode 655 arriving 10 ms after the first
     close, which is what puts the device to sleep today via the keylayout. But
     after the display goes off the keyboard node emits NOTHING — no 654 on
     open, and not even a 655 on the second close. The MCU gates its UART on the
     display/suspend state, so an input event can never be what wakes this
     device. The hall sensor is a *wakeup* sensor on the sensor DSP and is the
     only thing still reporting at that point.

     The keylayout keeps 655 -> SLEEP as the fast path for closing (it lands
     ~300 ms before this could); this file is what makes opening work at all.

     The Wakeup instance is deliberate: a non-wakeup sensor's events are only
     delivered once the AP is already awake, so opening the cover could never
     wake the device.

     Both the properties and the config_deviceStatesOnWhichTo{Sleep,WakeUp}
     arrays in FrameworkOverlayMalbec are needed — LogicalDisplayMapper
     branches on the deviceStatePropertyMigration() feature flag and reads a
     different one on each side.

     No PROPERTY_FOLDABLE_* here on purpose: this is a lid, not a fold. Adding
     them would make Jetpack WindowManager report a FoldingFeature to apps.
-->
<device-state-config>
    <device-state>
        <identifier>0</identifier>
        <name>OPENED</name>
        <properties>
            <property>com.android.server.policy.PROPERTY_POWER_CONFIGURATION_TRIGGER_WAKE</property>
        </properties>
        <flags/>
        <conditions>
            <sensor>
                <type>qti.sensor.hall_effect</type>
                <name>bu52053nvx Hall Effect Sensor Wakeup</name>
                <value>
                    <min-inclusive>0.5</min-inclusive>
                </value>
            </sensor>
        </conditions>
    </device-state>

    <device-state>
        <identifier>1</identifier>
        <name>CLOSED</name>
        <properties>
            <property>com.android.server.policy.PROPERTY_POWER_CONFIGURATION_TRIGGER_SLEEP</property>
        </properties>
        <flags/>
        <conditions>
            <sensor>
                <type>qti.sensor.hall_effect</type>
                <name>bu52053nvx Hall Effect Sensor Wakeup</name>
                <value>
                    <max>0.5</max>
                </value>
            </sensor>
        </conditions>
    </device-state>
</device-state-config>
